WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.340
- [Narrator] Good afternoon,
we begin with breaking news.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.520
- An explosion on an
oil drilling platform.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.450
- [Narrator] In the Gulf of Mexico.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.110
- [Narrator] On April 20th,
2010, an explosion occurred

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.600
on the Deep Water Horizon oil platform.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.180
- The worst oil spill in U.S. history.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.860
- [Narrator] You could see
that floating oil slick

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.840
from space every day.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.180
- [Narrator] And what followed
was millions of gallons

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.393
of oils spilling
uncontrollably into the ocean.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.480
- [Narrator] It is almost
unthinkable, isn't it?

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.450
- This is a battle we're
waging against an oil spill

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.973
that is assaulting our
shores and our citizens.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.610
- [Narrator] This video tells the story

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.960
of the race to stop that spill,
how engineers tried to pluck

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.680
a hole over 5,000 feet deep under water,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.980
and I wanted to know just how
much oil was actually lost

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.530
in the disaster, and try different methods

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.530
to better visualize the
sheer amount of loss

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.813
beyond just numbers alone.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.133
This is Deepwater Horizon Visualized.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.330
To visualize just how
much oil was spilled,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.330
we first need some sort
of measuring container,

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.940
but ordinary containers won't do,

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:12.330
so instead we will be using
the Empire State Building

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:13.563
in New York City.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.750
Theoretically, it can hold up
to 277 million gallons of oil,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.890
but to start off, our container is empty

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.133
as we look back to the
evening of the disaster.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.810
On the evening of April 20th, 2010,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.050
everything still seems
normal on Deepwater Horizon

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.113
with workers busy
finishing up the borehole.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.340
What many may not know is
that the Deepwater Horizon rig

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.780
isn't actually an oil platform
designed for extraction.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.370
Instead, it is a drilling
rig used on new sites.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.070
Its job is to create the initial borehole,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.310
and install a wellhead on the sea floor,

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.680
and once that is done, it'll move on

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.170
and another platform will take over

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.723
for the actual extraction of oil.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.960
The well itself is finished.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.260
The natural oil and gas
reservoir has been reached,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:15.960
and all that's left to
do now is create a seal,

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.900
so that Deepwater Horizon
can finally leave,

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.923
so that a smaller, less
costly rig can take its place.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.880
For this transition, it
is important that the well

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.970
is completely closed off,
and so the crew members

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.090
are carrying out a test to make
sure that the well is ready

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.813
to be temporarily abandoned for
the transition between rigs.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.250
But all of the sudden the
crew notices a huge spike

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.633
in pressure readings.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.200
This is bad because it usually indicates

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.550
that oil and gas has
unexpectedly entered the well.

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.730
The crew then runs the
same pressure test again,

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.713
but it is the same result.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.890
This is especially bad news

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.650
for oil giant, bp
considering it costs them

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:05.640
more than a million dollars
a day to operate the rig.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:07.710
And at this point, Deepwater Horizon

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.130
is already more than six
weeks behind schedule

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.440
to move to its next location.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.320
Economic pressures that may have resulted

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.960
in a culture of negligence.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.360
Despite the alarming pressure readings,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.613
the crew decides to carry on.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.090
And then just before 10:00 p.m.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.660
those still awake on board of
Deepwater Horizon are shocked

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.320
when a geyser of mud and
sea water begins gushing out

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.993
of the well and showering the rig.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.190
Before anyone can grasp
what is even happening,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.250
the worst case scenario occurs,

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.640
oil and gas escapes from
the wellhead and rushes up

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.913
to the platform where the gas explodes.

00:03:58.008 --> 00:03:59.490
- [News Reporter] We are
looking at new pictures

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:00.510
from the scene.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.310
The Coast Guard says
the explosion happened

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.840
around 10 o'clock last night.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.030
- There's a desperate search
in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.610
- For 11 people who were
on a drilling platform

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:09.813
when it exploded.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.970
- [Narrator] Of the 126
personnel initially on board

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.780
of Deepwater Horizon, 115 are rescued,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.970
but 11 men are missing.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.550
- The rig is still burning out of control.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.520
- [News Reporter] We have
covered approximately 1,940

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.533
square miles of search area.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.290
- [Narrator] A desperate
search is carried out

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:34.470
for the missing men, but
as the evening rolls over

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.920
into the next day and
the sun begins to rise,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.813
it isn't looking good.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.850
By day three of scouring the
Gulf and still no sign of them,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.893
the search is called off.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.530
Tragically, all 11 men were
presumed to be near the blast

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.173
and were unable to escape in time.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:02.880
The fate of Deepwater Horizon
is looking just as grim.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.970
Emergency responders attempt
to put out the blaze,

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.940
but this is made complicated
by its remote location

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.520
in the Gulf of Mexico, as well as oil

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.550
from the well continually
fueling the fire.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.830
- [Narrator] It was... You cannot imagine

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:20.790
how enormous this this rig is, anyway.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.880
It's the size of two or
three football fields.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.610
- [Narrator] There is little
anyone can realistically do

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:30.630
and any attempt to douse the
enormous fire is in vain.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.640
And while creaking and groaning,

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.033
the rig finally begins
to tilt into the gulf.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.260
After two days of burning,
it only takes minutes

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.530
for Deepwater Horizon to slip
beneath the water surface

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.020
and sink to the bottom of the sea.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.300
The fires come to an end,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.020
and yet it doesn't mark
the end of the disaster.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.080
In fact, this was still just a beginning

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.580
because deep down below,

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.040
the well Deepwater Horizon had drilled

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.470
is now leaking into the Gulf,

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:05.013
starting to fill our
measuring container with oil.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:09.990
Initially, bp reported

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.870
that no oil was escaping into the sea,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.480
but this was corrected
just a few days later

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.263
when a new report came out
that there was indeed a leak.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.100
As the extent of the spill
became more apparent,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.590
bp began feeling the immense pressure

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.080
to close the leak as soon as possible.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:30.030
And our first order of business

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.970
is to send a remote operated vehicle down

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:34.680
to the well itself.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.320
This vehicle should provide answers.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:40.320
and help them figure out
where exactly the oil

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.313
is leaking from.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.730
Every well drilled has a
very large complex structure

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.180
installed on the sea floor
called a blowout preventer

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:53.283
or BOP for short.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.190
The drill pipe from the rig

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.860
to the well must run through the BOP,

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.290
which is a safeguard to
prevent uncontrolled oil

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:05.490
and gas from traveling up the
drill pipe and to the rig.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:07.740
Essentially the BOP is there

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:12.150
to prevent exactly what
happened here, a blowout.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.450
To do so, the BOP has two
systems that can be used

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:19.650
to close a well, pipe ramps
which are heavy metal blocks

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.860
that can clamp together to stop the flow.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.780
And annular preventers

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:28.530
that close the pipe using a rubber seal.

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.360
And on the day of the accident,

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.210
crew members on the
Deepwater Horizon platform

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:37.560
did actually deploy both systems
within the BOP in an effort

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:38.823
to close the well.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:43.260
However, because these systems
were activated too late,

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:45.990
they didn't work as intended.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.550
Oil and gas had already
entered the pipe above the BOP,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:52.800
meaning there was nothing left to stop it

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.050
from reaching the rig.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.620
In theory, this also should
have been preventable

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:02.190
as there was one final
fail safe within the BOP

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:05.640
that should have been
engaged to close the well.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.250
The Blind Shear Ram.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:12.330
Once activated, the Blind Shear
Ram uses a sharp metal blade

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:16.050
to cut the pipe and form a seal.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.320
Crew members later reported
that they tried to activate

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.690
the Blind Shear Ram after the explosions,

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:25.470
but because they had lost
power, the switch didn't work.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:28.980
However, this also shouldn't
have mattered as the BOP

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:31.350
is designed with a fail safe mechanism

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:34.080
to automatically engage
the Blind Shear Ram

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.630
if it loses connection with the rig,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:39.660
such as in the case of a power outage.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.650
Why this didn't work is
a mystery to the experts,

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.773
and something the ROV will study.

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.970
And once they are able to reach the BOP,

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.180
a devastating site is waiting for them.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.290
The crews says that the
Blind Shear Ram did engage,

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.450
but because the pipe had buckled,

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.660
it did not cut the pipe as it should have.

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:06.360
Rather, the Ram punctured the pipe,

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:08.700
thereby making things even worse

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:12.303
and allowing a large volume
of oil and gas to escape.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.630
Now aware of the full
extent of the problem,

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.513
it was time to come up with a plan.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:20.760
The only guaranteed way

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.400
to permanently seal the
well will be by drilling

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.220
a completely new borehole that intersects

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.780
with the existing one.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.090
That way mud and cement can be pumped

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.520
through the new entrance to seal the leak,

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.033
eventually choking the flow
of oil and gas completely.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.080
The crew begins drilling this relief well

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.240
12 days after the explosion,

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.090
and a week later they
begin drilling a second one

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.760
just in case the first should fail.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.950
But this process will take months,

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:53.190
and every day until then, the
well will still be leaking,

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.830
causing massive environmental damage

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.553
and filling up our container
of the Empire State Building.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.000
Therefore, experts head
to the drawing board

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.250
to figure out alternative solutions

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:11.220
that could lead to a faster result,

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.413
and they come up with an idea.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.100
What if they could simply cover the leak

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.170
with some sort of dome.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.300
A structure like that exists

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:23.760
and it is called the Cofferdam,

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.310
a large steel and concrete structure,

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:29.250
weighing nearly 100 metric tons.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.883
A solution that has never
been tried before this deep.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.410
The Cofferdam is designed
with an opening on the side,

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:40.230
so that it can be lifted
over the broken pipe

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.580
and on the walls of the
Cofferdam there are four flaps.

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:46.980
These are there in case
the dam starts sinking

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:51.660
into the ocean mud to prevent
it from sinking too low.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:54.600
If everything goes to plan,
most of the oil would rush up,

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:57.570
and flow through a pipe
inserted into a smaller opening

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:58.950
at the top of the dome.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.380
And from there the oil
would be directed up

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:02.763
to a tanker ship.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:06.150
To turn this plan into reality,

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:08.370
crew members are slowly getting to work,

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:11.010
lowering the Cofferdam into the gulf,

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:14.493
but almost immediately there is a problem.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.100
As the Cofferdam descends

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.560
into the colder high pressure depths,

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:22.290
the gas escaping from the well reacts

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:26.160
with the surrounding water,
and forms methane hydrates,

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.730
essentially ice crystals that
block the opening at the top

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:30.563
of the dome.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:33.780
And with the opening frozen off,

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.680
the oil and gas inside the
dome now has nowhere to escape

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:39.810
and therefore the entire Cofferdam

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.120
becomes dangerously buoyant.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:45.630
Almost like a balloon, the
oil fills the Cofferdam

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:48.900
and makes it rush up
back towards the surface.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.300
The crew members above
are now staring down

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:53.070
a potential disaster

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:55.440
because as the Cofferdam
begins rushing up,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.060
it is heading straight towards

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.043
one of the response vessels above.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:03.870
Thankfully, the crew is able
to get it back under control

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:06.810
with enough time to
direct the Cofferdam away

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.400
from the vessel.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:10.980
Nonetheless, they have to abandon the dam,

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.130
which eventually sinks back
down to the bottom of the ocean

00:12:14.130 --> 00:12:16.203
where it will remain abandoned.

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.600
With this attempt a bust,
there's still no time

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.080
for frustration and the
engineers immediately jump back

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:26.730
to the drawing board.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:30.450
They come up with plan C, the pipe.

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.290
For this to work,

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:34.740
they will attempt to siphon
the oil and gas to a tanker,

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.680
but this time without a Cofferdam.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:41.040
Rather, they simply insert a smaller pipe

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.710
into the broken well pipe.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:47.190
And this method actually
works to a degree.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.560
The crew is able to start
collecting some of the oil

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:52.650
in a tank and flare the
natural gas overboard

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:56.463
by diverting it away from the
ship and setting it ablaze.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:01.110
The oil flow is finally slowed down.

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:04.110
Our measuring container
is filling up slower now,

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:06.480
but it is still steadily increasing

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.960
as oil leaks out around the pipe.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:14.960
And so experts move on yet
again with their next idea,

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:16.950
and they come up with their plan

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:19.053
for the so-called junk shot.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.770
To do this, a very complex procedure

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:25.500
is set up underwater near the well

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.030
and a new drilling vessel,

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:31.050
the Q4000 is positioned
above the leak site.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:34.590
Using this vessel's pipe heavy
drilling mud will be pumped

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:39.060
into the BOP along with
lots of different junk.

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:42.990
This includes anything from
golf balls to rubber tires,

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.910
or knotted rope.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:47.820
The idea is to use the
weight of the mud and junk

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:50.400
to force the oil back down into the well,

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:54.000
so that in a second step,
cement can be poured on top

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.250
in order to create a seal.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:57.720
- It's kinda like stopping up a toilet,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.820
plug up the blowout preventer,

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:01.710
and then at that point,
once we get it plugged up,

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.780
we can actually pump
heavy fluids down the well

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:05.400
and hopefully stop it from flowing

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.530
and then pump some cement after that.

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:08.940
- [Narrator] With fingers crossed,

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.460
crew members start the process.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.340
Mud and junk are shot
into the damaged well,

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:16.140
and using underwater cameras,

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:18.423
crew members monitor the situation.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.290
But yet again, nothing goes to plan,

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:26.280
as they watch the junk
get spewed right back out.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:29.670
The pressure from the
well is simply too high,

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:33.240
meaning any material dumped
on top is simply displaced

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.593
by the gushing oil.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:38.700
The crew attempts the junk
shot several more times,

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:41.040
but it is a futile effort.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.130
And experts warn that
if they continue trying,

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.350
it could lead to instability in the well,

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:49.950
causing what's known as
an underwater blowout.

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:52.380
This would mean that
oil may begin escaping

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:55.320
not just from the well,
but also from many points

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:57.600
throughout the surrounding sea floor.

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:01.860
If this happens, containment
will be virtually impossible,

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:04.833
and so they abandon the junk shot idea,

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:09.690
and suddenly it's back
to square one yet again.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.840
- [News Reporter] Cleanup
is costing $6 million a day,

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:13.920
and when it's all over,

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.280
the price will be over a billion dollars.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.340
- [Narrator] With the initial
borehole from plan A still far

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:21.840
from reaching their destination,

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:24.510
the engineers decide to revisit the idea

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:27.150
of a containment dome.

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:28.530
Even though it didn't work

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.950
with the original large
Cofferdam, this time they decide

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:35.040
to go for a much smaller,
better fitting cap

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:38.103
that could be installed
directly onto the BOP.

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:43.140
Rather than encasing the
entire area with a large dome,

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:47.250
they could direct the oil flow
straight through this cap,

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:48.813
but there is one issue.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:53.160
In order to do this, they will
need to cut the damaged pipe

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.147
that is still connected to the BOP.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.520
Up until this point, the kinks
in the pipe have been helping

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:02.940
to actually slow the rate
of oil that is leaking out.

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:04.350
And if it is cut off,

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:07.080
the rate of oil escaping
will likely increase

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:10.140
by as much as 20%.

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:13.380
It is an extremely risky move.

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.300
If this cap doesn't work,

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.720
then they will have made the
situation worse for nothing,

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:19.863
but they have to try.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:24.030
The decision is made to
cut the damaged pipe off

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:28.170
from the BOP, oil starts
rushing out at a faster pace

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:31.653
than ever before and the clock is ticking.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:37.560
It takes several days to
situate the new smaller cap

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:38.943
on top of the BOP.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.630
Having learned from the freezing issues

00:16:42.630 --> 00:16:44.460
with the previous Cofferdam,

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.650
this time warm water is pumped down

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:50.580
to hopefully prevent the
formation of ice crystals.

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:54.150
And as engineers watch the
underwater vehicles at work,

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:59.150
everyone halts their breath
and then with the cap in place,

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:03.960
the oil starts to flow and
everyone sighs with relief

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:05.943
as it actually works.

00:17:07.620 --> 00:17:09.630
The cap has created a seal,

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:12.720
and is collecting a
large portion of the oil,

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:16.620
sending it up to a collection
ship on the surface.

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:19.560
In fact, so much oil and gas are collected

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:21.630
that several more vessels are added

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:26.630
for storage and processing,
but the work still isn't done.

00:17:27.480 --> 00:17:30.240
Even though this is the most
progress experts have made

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:31.440
in stemming the flow,

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:34.500
they still aren't
capturing all of the leak.

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:38.853
And our container continues to
fill though at a slower rate.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:45.120
Throughout the next month,
engineers are able to devise,

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:48.210
and install a series
of better fitting caps,

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:51.930
each one lowering the amount
of oil escaping into the sea.

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:52.763
- The story today

00:17:52.763 --> 00:17:56.130
is that they're gonna literally
put a new cap, a new top

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.970
that may not be the right
nomenclature on the well

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.860
that's been leaking in the Gulf of Mexico

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:04.713
for upteen weeks and months or whatever.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:08.730
- [Narrator] Until finally a
tight enough cap is installed

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.430
that is able to completely
stop the oil from leaking

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:14.730
for the first time in three months.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:18.150
86 days after the initial explosion,

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:21.423
the oil finally stops
leaking into the ocean.

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:27.270
However, tensions are still
high as there is no telling

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:30.423
how long the temporary
capping can hold for.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:35.130
Luckily though, it does hold
for the time it is needed,

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:38.850
and eventually the first relief
well reaches its destination

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.043
intersecting with the original well.

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:45.570
Almost immediately, heavy
mud and cement are now poured

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:48.720
into the bottom of the well
and the news is delivered

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.260
to the world that the well has
officially been successfully

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.390
and permanently sealed.

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:57.993
110 days after the whole ordeal began.

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:04.620
In the end, approximately
134 million gallons of oil

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:06.990
were lost in the Gulf of Mexico,

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.620
filling our container to about 50%.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.187
And this is the figure prominently cited

00:19:13.187 --> 00:19:17.160
across a bunch of articles,
and yet this figure

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:19.140
is only a conservative estimate

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.960
of how much oil was truly spilled.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.060
A group of independent scientists,

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:27.600
and researchers came together
with their own assessment

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.210
and they measured the flow
of oil as seen from videos

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:33.240
of the leak and calculated
effects from pressure,

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:37.950
temperature, geological
conditions, and many other factors.

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:39.690
And according to their estimate,

00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:44.070
over 210 million gallons
of oil were actually lost

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:44.903
in the leak.

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.050
A massive increase from
the official calculation,

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:54.050
now filling our container up to about 75%.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:58.650
And this is just a pure crude oil,

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.030
though it is important to remember

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:04.290
that every gallon of oil can
coat huge areas of sea surface

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:07.650
as visible by this satellite
image taken by NASA,

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.683
showing the oil covered
sea in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.010
- [Lisa] We found that the animals

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:15.660
that lived in the near shore environment

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:17.910
had lower survival rates.

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:21.450
We saw 900 plus Dolphins strand,

00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:24.840
or wash ashore, many of them dead.

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:28.080
- [Narrator] Bp has heavily
contested this larger figure,

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:30.090
claiming that it doesn't take into account

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:32.880
the hundreds of thousands of
gallons that were siphoned

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:36.450
and collected before it could
enter the Gulf's waters.

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.310
Though it is also important to note

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.490
that bp has a vested interest
in keeping the figure

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:42.783
as low as possible.

00:20:44.014 --> 00:20:47.070
In the end, it is impossible
to know the true exact amount

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:49.800
of how much oil was
lost, but what we do know

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:52.500
is that no matter which of
the two figures you go with,

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:56.910
Deepwater Horizon was the
world's largest accidental spill

00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:00.630
to date, with irreputable
damage done to the ecosystem

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:03.870
and the loss of life of
11 men who were working

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:06.303
on the platform when it exploded.

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:12.420
Plugging a hole over
5,000 feet below the ocean

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:14.010
was a massive challenge,

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:16.260
but it's the kind of high stakes problem

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:19.410
only the best engineers can solve.

